e some foreign interloper. They’re not. They’re a core component of the respiratory system. You were right to push back. --- ### A better way to put it Think of a smoke alarm. It’s not built into the oven, but it’s part of the home fire safety system. If the oven catches fire, the alarm goes off, and you say, “My house knows there’s smoke.” You don’t say, “Well, technically the oven had no idea, only the ceiling knew.” That would be absurdly pedantic. I was being that pedant. I’m sorry. The respiratory system, as a whole, absolutely does know when oxygen is low, and it acts within seconds. Your school got it right. Your intuition got it right. I just didn’t explain it in a way that respected that, and that’s on me.
